WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.050 --> 00:00:02.963
Welcome back to Building
Global Secure Access.

2
00:00:02.963 --> 00:00:06.299
This is Chapter 3: the
client and its coverage.

3
00:00:06.299 --> 00:00:09.945
Every later policy assumes
the client is on the device,

4
00:00:09.945 --> 00:00:12.537
connected, current, and hard to turn off.

5
00:00:12.537 --> 00:00:16.637
This chapter measures that, device
by device and across the fleet.

6
00:00:16.637 --> 00:00:20.436
The guide already holds Learn's
Intune packaging and hardening,

7
00:00:20.436 --> 00:00:23.374
so we won't repeat them;
we'll build the measurement.

8
00:00:23.374 --> 00:00:25.889
The chapter is a draft awaiting review,

9
00:00:25.889 --> 00:00:29.800
and the code is illustrative,
tested against fixtures.

10
00:00:33.550 --> 00:00:34.913
Why measure the client?

11
00:00:34.913 --> 00:00:37.044
Because the compliant network check,

12
00:00:37.044 --> 00:00:40.688
the tenant restrictions and every
private app assume it's there.

13
00:00:40.688 --> 00:00:43.921
If the client is missing
or off, users are blocked,

14
00:00:43.921 --> 00:00:46.750
or traffic quietly goes around the policy.

15
00:00:46.750 --> 00:00:51.430
So the goal: say what share of in-scope
devices carry an active client;

16
00:00:51.430 --> 00:00:56.862
for each pilot device, say whether it runs
a current client with IPv4 preferred,

17
00:00:56.862 --> 00:01:00.874
DNS over HTTPS off and a healthy
connection; and from ring 2,

18
00:01:00.874 --> 00:01:04.300
show the hardening values,
with evidence for each.

19
00:01:04.300 --> 00:01:05.758
It's for the developers,

20
00:01:05.758 --> 00:01:10.260
the endpoint engineer who owns the
Intune app, and help desk leads.

21
00:01:14.010 --> 00:01:17.838
Learn's client page gives
the prerequisites: a joined,

22
00:01:17.838 --> 00:01:23.840
hybrid joined or registered device, though
registered devices carry only Private

23
00:01:23.840 --> 00:01:29.147
Access, in preview; an administrator to
install; IPv4 only; and no secure DNS.

24
00:01:29.147 --> 00:01:33.295
It gives the Intune values,
the DNS-over-HTTPS profile,

25
00:01:33.295 --> 00:01:39.798
four hardening registry values, and the
advice to let users run unrestricted first.

26
00:01:39.798 --> 00:01:43.590
For the fleet, the beta device
usage summary counts total,

27
00:01:43.590 --> 00:01:47.947
active and inactive devices, with
Network Access Policy Read All.

28
00:01:47.947 --> 00:01:54.484
Clients 1.8.239 or later use Universal
Continuous Access Evaluation.

29
00:01:54.484 --> 00:01:58.966
One difference: the summary's parameter
table marks traffic type required,

30
00:01:58.966 --> 00:02:02.140
while its own example and
the guide leave it out.

31
00:02:05.890 --> 00:02:07.115
What does it leave to you?

32
00:02:07.115 --> 00:02:10.099
The Validate commands
are typed on one device,

33
00:02:10.099 --> 00:02:12.768
with no record to compare across devices,

34
00:02:12.768 --> 00:02:17.715
so the kit's device script runs the same
reads, read-only, and writes one record.

35
00:02:17.715 --> 00:02:21.660
A device count means nothing without
the number that should be there,

36
00:02:21.660 --> 00:02:24.290
so coverage divides by
Intune's in-scope count.

37
00:02:24.290 --> 00:02:27.814
Hardening in the pilot hides who
needs to turn the client off,

38
00:02:27.814 --> 00:02:30.915
so it's information in the
pilot and a check from ring 2.

39
00:02:30.915 --> 00:02:35.365
An old client quietly loses a
protection, so there's a version check.

40
00:02:35.365 --> 00:02:38.390
And an exclusion without
an owner never ends,

41
00:02:38.390 --> 00:02:41.780
so exclusions live in
the exception register.

42
00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:47.005
Here are the seven checks.

43
00:02:47.005 --> 00:02:50.276
Coverage: active devices
over in-scope devices,

44
00:02:50.276 --> 00:02:54.805
against the target, ninety-eight
percent after ring 3 in the guide.

45
00:02:54.805 --> 00:02:58.185
Each device's client is at
least the minimum version,

46
00:02:58.185 --> 00:03:01.330
here the one Universal
access evaluation needs.

47
00:03:01.330 --> 00:03:05.580
IPv4 is preferred, the value
Learn's install script sets.

48
00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:09.805
DNS over HTTPS is off in Edge and Chrome.

49
00:03:09.805 --> 00:03:11.043
Health is connected.

50
00:03:11.043 --> 00:03:14.154
The four hardening values
are set, from ring 2.

51
00:03:14.154 --> 00:03:18.692
And each client exclusion has an
owner, an approver and an expiry.

52
00:03:18.692 --> 00:03:23.473
One honest note: the browser policy
value the script expects is Chromium's,

53
00:03:23.473 --> 00:03:27.760
not stated on the Learn page read,
and the research gaps say so.

54
00:03:31.510 --> 00:03:32.548
Who owns this?

55
00:03:32.548 --> 00:03:35.777
The endpoint engineer owns the Intune app,

56
00:03:35.777 --> 00:03:40.022
the DNS-over-HTTPS profile,
and the hardening from ring 2.

57
00:03:40.022 --> 00:03:44.809
The help desk uses the client's collect
logs and health check before escalating.

58
00:03:44.809 --> 00:03:47.147
The policy owner approves exclusions,

59
00:03:47.147 --> 00:03:51.310
and the security officer keeps the
runs and the health check exports.

60
00:03:51.310 --> 00:03:55.985
The fleet read needs only Network
Access Policy Read All, delegated.

61
00:03:55.985 --> 00:04:00.760
The device script runs as an administrator
on a test device and only reads.

62
00:04:00.760 --> 00:04:05.960
And the in-scope count comes from Intune,
with its date, in the settings file.

63
00:04:09.710 --> 00:04:12.785
Lab 1, in the kit's folder, no tenant.

64
00:04:12.785 --> 00:04:16.423
Step 1 lists one read,
the device usage summary.

65
00:04:16.423 --> 00:04:19.119
Step 2 reads the pilot tenant's summary:

66
00:04:19.119 --> 00:04:22.573
eighteen devices, fifteen
active, three inactive.

67
00:04:22.573 --> 00:04:25.931
Step 3 reads the four pilot
devices' client records,

68
00:04:25.931 --> 00:04:28.010
as the device script wrote them.

69
00:04:28.010 --> 00:04:34.348
Pilot 3 runs an older client with
DisabledComponents zero, so IPv6 is preferred;

70
00:04:34.348 --> 00:04:39.340
pilot 4 is disconnected; and only
pilot 1 has the hardening values.

71
00:04:43.090 --> 00:04:45.177
Step 4 runs the client checks.

72
00:04:45.177 --> 00:04:48.290
Coverage is eighty-three
percent against ninety-eight.

73
00:04:48.290 --> 00:04:55.765
Pilot 3 has an old client, IPv6 preferred,
and DNS over HTTPS still on in Chrome.

74
00:04:55.765 --> 00:04:57.727
Pilot 4 is disconnected.

75
00:04:57.727 --> 00:05:02.053
The Hyper-V exclusion has an
owner, an approver and an expiry.

76
00:05:02.053 --> 00:05:04.889
Hardening is only
information in the pilot.

77
00:05:04.889 --> 00:05:10.186
Step 5 asks the same devices ring 2's
question, where hardening is required:

78
00:05:10.186 --> 00:05:14.880
three devices fail, because only
pilot 1 has the remediation.

79
00:05:18.630 --> 00:05:21.130
Step 6 reads the device script's header.

80
00:05:21.130 --> 00:05:26.013
It runs on a Windows device, not on
the lab laptop, as an administrator,

81
00:05:26.013 --> 00:05:30.808
and only reads: the client's file
version, the four hardening values,

82
00:05:30.808 --> 00:05:35.080
the IPv4 preference and the
browsers' DNS-over-HTTPS policy.

83
00:05:35.080 --> 00:05:39.160
The health is read from the tray's
health check by a person and passed in,

84
00:05:39.160 --> 00:05:40.918
because the script can't see it.

85
00:05:40.918 --> 00:05:45.380
Step 7 runs this chapter's tests:
three passed, none failed.

86
00:05:45.380 --> 00:05:47.580
Then delete the output folder.

87
00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:56.749
Lab 2 needs one Windows 11 test device,
joined to Entra ID and enrolled in Intune,

88
00:05:56.749 --> 00:06:00.418
with the client installed
from the guide's Intune app.

89
00:06:00.418 --> 00:06:02.613
On the device, open the health check,

90
00:06:02.613 --> 00:06:05.567
then run the device script
as an administrator.

91
00:06:05.567 --> 00:06:08.755
Run the guide's own reads
beside it as a cross-check.

92
00:06:08.755 --> 00:06:12.255
Add the record to your
inputs, on agency storage.

93
00:06:12.255 --> 00:06:14.567
Then read the fleet's count and check.

94
00:06:14.567 --> 00:06:18.853
If the service reports no active
devices while the client is connected,

95
00:06:18.853 --> 00:06:23.355
record it: it may be the traffic type
parameter Learn's table marks required.

96
00:06:23.355 --> 00:06:26.393
Nothing changed, so
backing out is signing out.

97
00:06:26.393 --> 00:06:28.760
The authors haven't run this lab.

98
00:06:32.510 --> 00:06:35.007
Coverage against Intune's count supports

99
00:06:35.007 --> 00:06:37.860
system monitoring and
the component inventory.

100
00:06:37.860 --> 00:06:41.710
Each device's configuration
supports configuration settings.

101
00:06:41.710 --> 00:06:44.938
Hardening from ring 2
supports least functionality,

102
00:06:44.938 --> 00:06:47.285
and governed exclusions support both.

103
00:06:47.285 --> 00:06:50.513
The indicators are FedRAMP's,
mapped by the program,

104
00:06:50.513 --> 00:06:52.973
and the assessor decides the controls.

105
00:06:52.973 --> 00:06:58.449
The dangers: hardening in the pilot, which
hides who needs to turn the client off;

106
00:06:58.449 --> 00:07:04.348
IPv6 preferred or DNS over HTTPS on,
which lets traffic go around the client;

107
00:07:04.348 --> 00:07:06.960
and a raw count mistaken for coverage.

108
00:07:06.960 --> 00:07:11.032
You're done when the count comes from
Intune with its date, every pilot

109
00:07:11.032 --> 00:07:15.247
device has a record, and coverage
meets the target before the ring exits.

110
00:07:15.247 --> 00:07:19.570
Next, Chapter 4: Microsoft
traffic, and change plans.
